---
import ReviewLayout from "../../layouts/ReviewLayout.astro"
import { reviewHref } from "../../lib/nav"

const headings = [
  { depth: 2, slug: "memory", text: "Memory tied to the codebase" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "lsp", text: "Language-server tools, as an add-on" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "skills", text: "Review skills by path" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "preloading", text: "Deterministic context preloading" },
]
---

<ReviewLayout
  title="Planned"
  description="What is not built yet. Everything on this page is a direction, not a feature: no flag, export or file of the package does any of it today."
  current="planned"
  headings={headings}
>
  <div class="rv-note planned">
    <p>
      <span class="rv-tag planned">planned</span> Each section below is planned work, not shipped. The pages before
      this one describe only what the code at the repository's main branch does.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="memory">Memory tied to the codebase</h2>
  <p><span class="rv-tag planned">planned</span></p>
  <p>
    Today every review starts from nothing but the repository's instruction files. The plan is a memory of the
    codebase's own rules, the kind a maintainer repeats in review after review, kept as records on the kernel's
    journal, the same journal every turn already writes. It would learn from merged pull requests: what reviewers asked
    for and what was changed because of it. And evals would decide whether a remembered rule earns its place in the
    prompt, before it ever does.
  </p>

  <h2 id="lsp">Language-server tools, as an add-on</h2>
  <p><span class="rv-tag planned">planned</span></p>
  <p>
    The six tools read text: lines, a search, a glob, a diff. A language server answers what text cannot, such as where
    a name is defined and every place that refers to it. The plan is tools backed by <code>tsgo</code>'s language
    server, for definitions and references, as an add-on beside the six, not a part of the core: a host that wants them
    plugs them in, and an eval shows whether they help. Today the turn's tool list is fixed (see
    <a href={reviewHref("plugins") + "#not-yet"}>what cannot be swapped yet</a>), so this needs that list to open up
    first.
  </p>

  <h2 id="skills">Review skills by path</h2>
  <p><span class="rv-tag planned">planned</span></p>
  <p>
    Instruction files apply by folder. The plan is review skills attached to paths: what to look for in a migration,
    a sandbox, a public API, given to a turn only when its group holds files those paths match, so a turn reads the
    guidance for the code in front of it and nothing else.
  </p>

  <h2 id="preloading">Deterministic context preloading</h2>
  <p><span class="rv-tag planned">planned</span></p>
  <p>
    A finder turn spends its first steps reading: each file under review whole, then what its changed lines call and
    are called by. Much of that is predictable. The plan is to have code, not the model, load that context before the
    turn starts, the same way every time for the same pull request, so the turn's steps go to judging instead of
    fetching.
  </p>
</ReviewLayout>
